*                      and could not be reached by an API session at all.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    call !peer_local(is.local, peer.addr)
*
*    is.local   @true  the peer is this machine
*               @false the peer is somewhere else, OR no peer address was found
*    peer.addr  the address it decided on, '' when there was none
*
*    status()  0  a peer address was found and classified
*              1  no peer address at all - a console session, or a transport
*                 that reports none
*
*    ***THIS IS ONE RULE AND IT NOW HAS TWO CALLERS, WHICH IS WHY IT IS A FILE
*    AND NOT A GOSUB.***  It was LOGIN's internal peer.local.test until 5 Sep
*    2026.  APISRVR needs the same decision and CANNOT get it from there:
*    APISRVR is the top-level program for an API connection, so CPROC never
*    runs and CPROC's "call '$LOGIN'" never happens.  Copying the case
*    statement into APISRVR would have made two copies of one security rule -
*    SDADMIN's header names that shape and what it has already cost.
*
*    ***BOTH TRANSPORTS ARE READ, AND "else" WOULD BE THE BUG.***  ssh reports
*    the peer in SSH_CLIENT; the API reports it in system(42), which is
*    ip_addr, filled by getpeername() in linuxio.c when the connection is
*    accepted.  A session is one or the other, so reading both costs nothing
*    while ASSUMING which one applies is how this would rot the next time a
*    transport is added.  ssh is read first because it is the transport this
*    was written for.
*
*    ***AN EMPTY ADDRESS IS NOT LOCAL, AND THAT DIRECTION IS DELIBERATE.***
*    The caller decides what to do about it, but this never answers @true on
*    an absence.  For an API session there is ALWAYS a peer - it is a socket -
*    so an empty address there means something is wrong rather than something
*    is local, and refusing is the safe direction.  Same rule as APISRVR's
*    "a missing sdapi group refuses everybody", and the opposite of NEWVOC's
*    tier lists; do not carry that convention across.  A CONSOLE session has
*    no peer either, which is why LOGIN's gate tests K$INTERACTIVE beside this
*    rather than relying on the answer here.
*
*    ***WHAT "LOCAL" MEANS IS LOOPBACK, NOT "THE SAME COMPUTER".***  A
*    connection to this machine's own LAN address is NOT local by this test,
*    and that is the intended reading: it travelled over the network and
*    anything else on the network could have sent it.  The b122 witness for
*    PRE_RELEASE 167 rests on exactly that - it refused a self-connection to
*    the host's own 10.0.0.3 while the console was admitted.
*
*    ***AND IT DOES NOT SEE THROUGH AN ssh TUNNEL, WHICH IS A REAL LIMIT AND
*    IS WRITTEN DOWN RATHER THAN ASSUMED AWAY.***  "ssh -L" terminates on this
*    host, so a tunnelled API connection is accepted FROM 127.0.0.1 and this
*    answers @true for it.  Closing that is an sshd matter (AllowTcpForwarding,
*    or a Match block), not something a peer address can do - see
*    PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 170.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
$flags trusted

subroutine PEER_LOCAL(is.local, peer.addr)
$catalog !peer_local

$include keys.h

   set.status 0

   is.local = @false
   peer.addr = ''

*  ssh first: it is the transport this exists for.
   peer.addr = trim(field(env('SSH_CLIENT'), ' ', 1))

*  Then the API.  Not "else" - see the header.
   if peer.addr = '' then peer.addr = trim(system(42))

   if peer.addr = '' then
      set.status 1
      return
   end

   begin case
      case peer.addr = '::1'
         is.local = @true
      case peer.addr = '::ffff:127.0.0.1'
         is.local = @true
      case field(peer.addr, '.', 1) = '127'
         is.local = @true
   end case

   set.status 0
   return

* END-CODE
